# Kconfig for the beeper module.
#
# The second of the lab's two local modules. tilt_filter shows a module that
# is pure arithmetic; this one shows a module that owns a pair of devices and
# a thread, which is the more realistic shape. Both are reached the same way:
# a directory named in ZEPHYR_EXTRA_MODULES, a symbol set in prj.conf.

menuconfig BEEPER
	bool "Touch feedback tones"
	depends on I2S && AUDIO_CODEC
	help
	  Short tones played through the board's TLV320DAC3100 codec and
	  onboard speaker when a slider is released.

	  The module owns i2s0 and the codec node outright: nothing else in
	  the application touches either. Set BEEPER=n and the application
	  still builds and runs, silently.

if BEEPER

config BEEPER_TONE_HZ
	int "Release tone frequency (Hz)"
	range 100 8000
	default 1760
	help
	  Frequency of the tone played by beeper_click(), when a control is
	  released.

	  1760 Hz is A6 - high enough to read as a UI tick rather than a
	  beep, and comfortably inside the small speaker's usable range.
	  The upper limit is the Nyquist frequency of the 16 kHz sample
	  rate; approaching it will alias audibly.

config BEEPER_TONE_MS
	int "Release tone duration (ms)"
	range 10 80
	default 40
	help
	  Duration of the tone played by beeper_click().

	  The whole tone is generated and queued before the transmitter is
	  started, so the upper limit is set by how much the module's memory
	  slab holds (see BEEPER_BLOCK_COUNT in src/beeper.c).

config BEEPER_PRESS_HZ
	int "Press tone frequency (Hz)"
	range 100 8000
	default 880
	help
	  Frequency of the tone played by beeper_press(), when a control is
	  first touched.

	  880 Hz is A5 - exactly one octave below the release tone. The
	  octave is why the pair sounds like one gesture rather than two
	  unrelated beeps; try moving it to 900 to hear how much that
	  relationship is doing.

config BEEPER_PRESS_MS
	int "Press tone duration (ms)"
	range 10 80
	default 25
	help
	  Duration of the tone played by beeper_press().

	  Shorter than the release tone on purpose. The press is an
	  acknowledgement, the release is the result, and making the first
	  lighter than the second is what keeps a drag from sounding like a
	  stutter.

config BEEPER_VOLUME_PERCENT
	int "Tone amplitude (percent of full scale)"
	range 1 100
	default 25
	help
	  Peak amplitude of the generated tone, as a percentage of 16-bit
	  full scale.

	  This is the digital level. Analogue gain is separate and lives in
	  the devicetree as the codec node's speaker-gain property - two
	  different knobs for the same thing, which is worth knowing about
	  before reaching for either.

endif # BEEPER
